'''augurism†''' (â′gėr-izm), ''n.'' [< ''augur'' + ''-ism.''] Augury.

'''augurist†''' (â′gėr-ist), ''n.'' [< ''augur'' + ''-ist.'' Cf. ''augurize.''] An augur.

'''augurize†''' (â′gėr-īz), ''v. t.'' or ''i.'' [< ''augur'' + ''-ize.''] To augur; act as an augur.

'''augurous†''' (â′gėr-us), ''a.'' [< ''augur'' + ''-ous.''] Predicting; foretelling; foreboding.
{{smaller block|Presaging in their ''augurous'' hearts.
{{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, xviii. 191.}}}}

'''augurship''' (â′gėr-ship), ''n.'' [< ''augur'' + ''-ship.''] The office or period of office of an augur.

'''augury''' (â′gū-ri), ''n.''; pl. ''auguries'' (-riz). [< ME. ''augury'', < OF. ''augurie'' (ME. also ''augure'', < OF. ''augure'') = Sp. Pg. It. ''augurio'', < L. ''augurium'', divination, prognostication, omen, < ''augur'', augur: see ''augur'', ''n.''] '''1.''' The art or practice of foretelling events by signs or omens.
{{smaller block|She knew by ''augury'' divine.
{{right|''Swift'', Cadenus and Vanessa.}}
The throne and sceptre of Ithaca were to be disposed by ''augury'', by the will of Jove, signified by some omen.
{{right|''J. Adams'', Works, IV. 571.}}}}
'''2.''' That which forebodes; that from which a prediction is drawn; an omen or significant token.
{{smaller block|Sad ''auguries'' of winter thence she drew.
{{right|''Dryden'', Hind and Panther, iii. 441.}}
I hail this interchange of sentiment . . . as an ''augury'' that . . . the peace and friendship which now exist between the two nations will be . . . perpetual.
{{right|''Lincoln'', in Raymond, p. 462.}}}}
'''3.''' Figuratively, indication; presage; promise.
{{smaller block|His diligence at school . . . gave ''augury'' of his future}}

