'''august<sup>1</sup>''' (â-gust′), ''a.'' [= F. ''auguste'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''augusto'', < L. ''augustus'', venerable, worthy of honor (assumed as a title by Octavius Cæsar and his successors), perhaps orig. ‘consecrated by augury,’ < ''augur'', augur (cf. ''robust'', < L. ''robustus'', < ''robur''); but usually associated with ''augere'', increase, extol: see ''auction''.] '''1.''' Inspiring reverence and admiration; majestic; solemnly grand or stately; sublime; magnificent; imposing.
{{smaller block|<poem>There is on earth a yet ''auguster'' thing,
Veiled though it be, than parliament or king.</poem>{{right|''Wither.''}}}}
{{smaller block|That ''august'' face of Truth. {{right|''Whittier'', Eve of Election.}}}}
{{smaller block|This was an extensive and magnificent structure, the creation of the prince’s own eccentric yet ''august'' taste.{{right|''Poe'', Tales, I. 339.}}}}
'''2.''' Venerable; worshipful; eminent. {{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Stately'', etc. (see ''majestic''), awful, imposing.}}

